00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:57.020
In 2015, the country with the largest proven
oil reserves on earth — enough to meet all

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:03.239
of America’s demand for forty years — ran
out of toilet paper.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:06.860
Hotels even asked guests to bring their own.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:13.240
Facing shortages of rice, sugar, milk, and
shampoo, on top of rolling blackouts, 30 million

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.650
people struggled to survive without the most
basic of basic goods.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:24.960
Venezuela, once the richest country in Latin
America, was experiencing the worst peacetime

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.860
economic crisis in modern history.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:35.110
A third of its entire economy disappeared
in one year alone — the same number as Ukraine

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.970
in 2022.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:43.040
Since then, 7 million people — a quarter
of the entire population — have fled the

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.150
country.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:50.689
This is the equivalent of the Great Depression,
the hyperinflation of Zimbabwe, and the Syrian

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:54.160
migrant crisis — all at the same time.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.759
And it’s happening in a country with 300
billion barrels of oil.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:06.850
That’s 11 times more than China and nearly
four times that of Russia, a place once referred

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.330
to as “more a gas station than a country”.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.680
Even this understates its good fortune.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.330
Venezuelan crude is practically begging to
be extracted.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.550
It’s not far off-shore like Norway’s.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.260
It’s not way up in the arctic, like Russia’s.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.800
And it’s not deep underground, like American
shale.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.480
Instead, it just naturally bubbles to the
surface.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.239
And, as the Gulf States prove, one does not
need smart technocratic governance, liberal

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:43.870
democracy, or even remotely favorable geography.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:50.760
Oil and oil alone can turn the barren Arabian
Desert into the Swiss Alps.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.900
So, how did Venezuela go so terribly wrong?

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:07.140
In 2013, a staggering 96% of Venezuelan exports
were related to petroleum.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:12.810
It’s not hard to see how oil prices could
make or break its economy.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.980
Lower prices, lower revenue.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.040
It’s very simple.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.520
Less intuitive is the effect this has on its
currency.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.040
You see, it’s not just about how much money
it generates.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.560
It’s also about what kind.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.909
When oil prices are high, there’s more demand
for Venezuela’s currency — the Bolivar

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:37.930
— so it appreciates.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:44.659
And the more one Bolivar is worth, the fewer
you can get for the same Dollar or Euro or

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:49.340
Yen, making its exports more expensive for
the rest of the world.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:54.800
Say, for instance, 10 Bolivars were worth
one U.S. Dollar.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.460
At this rate, someone in Florida could buy
a pound of 20 Bolivar Venezuelan coffee for

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.349
two USD.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:04.049
Not bad.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:08.270
But then, oil prices cause the Bolivar to
appreciate.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:13.629
That same pound of coffee now costs four U.S.
Dollars.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:18.949
Through no fault of their own, coffee farmers
are now half as globally competitive.

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:23.860
Now, this isn’t necessarily as bad as it
sounds.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:30.150
When exports are effectively twice as expensive,
imports are effectively 50% off.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:35.889
Those 10 original Bolivars are worth two dollars,
not just one.

00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:41.080
It’s no wonder virtually everything in Venezuela
is imported.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:46.240
From appliances and cars to simple things
like food and apparel.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.100
Even Bolivars themselves arrive on 747s.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:56.130
All of this is to say: in Venezuela, the highs
are high.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:00.820
Strong oil prices not only bring in loads
of cash, but they also make foreign goods

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.919
that much cheaper.

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:09.660
In 1970, Venezuela had a higher per capita
GDP than either Spain or Greece.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.169
Nearly 60% of the population was middle-class.

00:05:12.169 --> 00:05:17.449
And the Concorde flew direct flights to Paris.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:22.630
Another explosion of wealth came during China’s
rise in the early 2000s.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.479
As demand for oil soared, the country even
began importing doctors.

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:34.710
But with such high highs come extraordinarily
low lows.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.770
Because no smart politician withholds riches
from voters, nothing is ever set aside for

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.630
a rainy day.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:46.500
And in the oil business, there are always
rainy days.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:52.530
Remember: When oil prices fall, the state
both loses revenue, and the imports they’ve

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.020
come to depend on become more expensive.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.770
That’s a brutal combination.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:04.210
Those coffee farmers from earlier go bankrupt
when oil prices are high, leaving Venezuela

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:10.210
without domestic producers of… frankly,
anything, when they aren’t.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.440
This cycle of boom and bust has been going
on since the first well was drilled over a

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.270
hundred years ago.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:21.740
But this latest crisis is especially prolonged
thanks to a potent mix of weak oil prices

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:27.849
and U.S. sanctions, imposed after its president
manipulated elections.

00:06:27.849 --> 00:06:34.599
Things got so bad that the country once ran
out of money to pay its supplier of that money.

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:37.660
So what was the government’s solution?

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:38.670
Print.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:39.690
More.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:40.700
Money!

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.319
Since 2007, the central bank of Venezuela
is no longer independent, giving politicians

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:49.009
direct control of the money supply.

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:54.910
This, to everyone’s surprise, led to something
called “inflation”.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:59.319
At first, the government simply pretended
it wasn’t happening, refusing to release

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:00.990
the data.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:07.259
By the time it finally gave up the charade
in 2019, inflation was at 130… thousand

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:16.040
percent — about 129,997% higher than its
neighbor, Colombia.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.949
While taxi drivers can easily raise their
rates to adjust for inflation, the salaries

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:24.509
of professionals were reduced to nothing.

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:29.790
No wonder such a high proportion of migrants
were doctors, lawyers, and professors.

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.419
In Venezuela, they were effectively working
for free.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:39.009
And when your savings today will surely become
worthless tomorrow, there’s no reason not

00:07:39.009 --> 00:07:42.160
to spend your paycheck immediately.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:47.320
When they ran out of cash, people simply took
out loans to buy everything from TVs to plastic

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.320
surgery.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.259
So, the government took another page out of
the “just-print-more-money” school of

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:56.139
economic thought — price controls.

00:07:56.139 --> 00:08:02.229
After all, if prices are too high, why not
just impose a ceiling?

00:08:02.229 --> 00:08:06.820
In a sense, it works: state-run supermarkets
are cheap.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:09.000
Ridiculously cheap.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.080
The question is: is there anything inside?

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:18.180
Because prices are set well below market rates,
shoppers are incentivized to buy as much as

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.940
they can get their hands on.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:26.129
Anything you don’t need can easily be resold
for twice as much across the street or twenty

00:08:26.129 --> 00:08:28.409
times as much across the border.

00:08:28.409 --> 00:08:36.120
It’s estimated that up to 14% of Venezuela’s
highly-subsidized gas is smuggled internationally.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.560
In 2015, lines outside grocery stores got
so long that everyone was assigned one day

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:47.000
of the week for shopping, based on the last
digit of their national ID.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.480
To prevent the same person from using their
quota at multiple stores, fingerprint scanners

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.870
were installed at checkouts.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.680
Soldiers even patrolled the aisles.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:01.620
Here, once again, the government had a solution.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.750
In Venezuela, there is no one exchange rate.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:09.570
The value of your money depends on who you
are.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.620
What could possibly go wrong?

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:17.310
To encourage companies to import essential
goods in short supply, for example, it offers

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.709
a special, favorable exchange rate.

00:09:20.709 --> 00:09:25.820
The way it works is that a foreign company,
say a Brazilian rice producer, wants to be

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:30.370
paid in the global currency of trade — dollars.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:35.790
For a Venezuelan company to buy and import
that rice, therefore, it has to somehow get

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:37.730
its hands on some USD.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:43.550
That’s a problem because, until recently,
the government carefully restricted access

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.649
to foreign currency.

00:09:45.649 --> 00:09:49.940
Unlike most countries, you couldn’t just
walk down to your neighborhood currency exchange

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.260
and trade some cash.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:57.519
Huge multinational corporations like Pepsi
had to apply for the chance to exchange their

00:09:57.519 --> 00:10:00.910
Bolivars for U.S. Dollars.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.950
Even when these applications were accepted
— and they often weren’t — the money

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:10.960
might take months to arrive, making it hard
for companies to import supplies.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:17.190
So few new cars entered the country, for example,
that used ones became highly prized commodities.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.140
But, it gets worse.

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:24.740
The exchange rate offered by the government
wasn’t the “real” value of the Bolivar.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:30.750
Meaning, the government might give you one
USD for 5 Bolivars, but nobody else would

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:35.120
give you a Dollar for anything less than,
say, 50.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.800
The official exchange rate was essentially
a government handout — free money for those

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.360
smart or well-connected enough to get it.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.190
By approving these requests for Dollars, politicians
could reward their friends and family.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:53.889
By rejecting them, they could punish or silence
critics.

00:10:53.889 --> 00:10:59.260
It also opened the door to a different, more
sophisticated, form of arbitrage.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:03.829
You might ask the government for permission
to exchange 5 million Bolivars for one million

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:07.820
U.S. dollars to help ease the shortage of
car parts.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:12.209
But instead of using that money to import
a million dollars worth of tires, you might

00:11:12.209 --> 00:11:15.140
only import 500,000.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:20.019
You could then sell the other half million
dollars on the black market for far more than

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:23.900
the 2.5 million Bolivars you paid.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:28.310
And just like that, you’ve created money
out of thin air.

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:32.370
According to the government, you’re the
reason the system is broken.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:37.810
“If only people bought from stores what
they needed and nothing more.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:41.660
If only people stopped exchanging foreign
currency.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:47.350
If only they saved and invested rather than
spending everything they have.”

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:53.040
At the root of Venezuela’s problems, the
government claims, are individual opportunists.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:58.600
A similar argument is that the country is
plagued by a culture of selfish consumption

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.120
over prudent, long-term investment.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:07.110
Voters favor politicians who promise handouts
over those with the foresight to save for

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.070
the future.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:14.290
After decades of gross mismanagement, Venezuelans
have lost faith in the collective power of

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.130
the state.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:21.550
But if growing the pie is an unrealistic pipe
dream, the only rational response is to fight

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.560
for your and your family’s slice.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.680
As the world enters a period of financial
tightening and possibly even recession, today’s

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:36.180
sponsor, Brilliant dot org, can help us learn
from Venezuela by investing in our education

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.550
and diversifying our skill sets.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:43.459
When times are tough, people invest in themselves.

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:49.699
After the 2008 recession, U.S. college enrollment
increased by about two and a half million.

